BIO='''<span class="eyebrow">Biography</span><h2>Steady advocacy, grounded in Tulsa</h2>
<p>Claire Sampson grew up in midtown Tulsa, the daughter of a schoolteacher and a small-business owner. In the story of this firm, she watched close family friends spend years and most of their savings in a bitter divorce, and decided early that families deserved a better way through.</p>
<p>After earning an economics degree and a law degree, Claire spent her early career at a mid-sized firm handling contested custody and complex property cases. She learned that thorough preparation was the surest path to fair settlements, and that clients make better decisions when they truly understand their options.</p>
<p>She founded Sampson Family Law to build a practice around those lessons: a small caseload, direct attorney access, written plans and budgets, and a mediation-first philosophy backed by courtroom readiness. Her economics background makes her especially attentive to retirement accounts, business valuations, and the long-term tax consequences of settlements.</p>
<h2>Approach</h2><ul class="checks"><li>Every client receives a written roadmap after the first meeting</li><li>Agreement where it is safe and realistic; trial preparation where it is not</li><li>Parenting plans built around the child's actual routine</li><li>Financial analysis that looks five years ahead, not just to the decree</li></ul>
<h2>Outside the office</h2><p>Claire volunteers with a (fictional) Saturday legal information clinic, coaches youth soccer, and can usually be found on weekends walking the trails along the Arkansas River.</p>'''

POSTS=[
dict(slug="preparing-for-a-divorce-consultation",title="Preparing for Your First Divorce Consultation",cat="Divorce",read=5,img="signing.jpg",
dek="What to bring, what to ask, and how to make the most of your first meeting with a family law attorney.",
body='''<p>The first meeting with a family law attorney can feel overwhelming. A little preparation turns it from an anxious conversation into a productive planning session.</p>
<h2>Bring the paperwork you have</h2><p>You do not need a perfect file. Recent pay stubs, the last two years of tax returns, statements for bank and retirement accounts, mortgage and loan statements, and any court papers you have received are a strong start. If you have children, a rough sketch of the current weekly schedule helps too.</p>
<h2>Write down your goals and worries</h2><p>Before the meeting, list what matters most to you: staying in the house, a particular parenting schedule, protecting a business, or simply ending things quickly. Then list your biggest fears. A good attorney will address both.</p>
<h2>Questions worth asking</h2><ul><li>What are the likely paths for a case like mine, and how long does each take?</li><li>What decisions do I need to make soon, and which can wait?</li><li>How are fees structured, and what would a realistic budget look like?</li><li>What should I avoid doing while the case is pending?</li></ul>
<h2>Think about safety and privacy</h2><p>If you share devices or accounts with your spouse, consider how you will communicate privately with your attorney. If you have any safety concerns, raise them at the very beginning of the meeting.</p>
<blockquote>Preparation does not mean having every answer. It means arriving ready to ask the right questions.</blockquote>'''),
dict(slug="understanding-oklahoma-child-support",title="Understanding Child Support in Oklahoma",cat="Child Support",read=6,img="law-books.jpg",
dek="A plain-English look at how Oklahoma's income-shares guidelines work and why the details matter.",
body='''<p>Child support often feels like a mysterious number produced by a computer. In reality, Oklahoma's guidelines follow a logical framework, and understanding it helps parents spot errors and plan ahead.</p>
<h2>The income-shares idea</h2><p>Oklahoma's guidelines start by combining both parents' incomes and estimating what an intact household at that income level would typically spend on a child. That total obligation is then divided between the parents in proportion to their incomes.</p>
<h2>What gets added or adjusted</h2><p>Health insurance premiums for the child, work-related child care, and the number of overnights each parent has can all change the result. Income disputes, particularly for self-employed parents, are often the biggest source of disagreement.</p>
<h2>The national picture</h2><p>According to the U.S. Census Bureau, custodial parents were due $29.9 billion in child support in 2022 and received $19.2 billion. That gap is one reason enforcement tools exist. See the <a href="../evidence.html">evidence page</a> for sources.</p>
<h2>When things change</h2><p>A significant change in income or in a child's needs may justify a modification. Because changes generally apply only from the date a request is filed, waiting can be costly.</p>
<p>Oklahoma Human Services offers an <a href="https://oklahoma.gov/okdhs/services/child-support-services.html">official child support services program</a> that can help establish, modify, and enforce orders.</p>'''),
dict(slug="co-parenting-through-the-holidays",title="Co-Parenting Through the Holidays",cat="Child Custody",read=4,img="gathering-place.jpg",
dek="Practical ways separated parents can reduce conflict and protect children's joy during the busiest season of the year.",
body='''<p>Holidays magnify everything about co-parenting: the logistics, the emotions, and the stakes for children who want to enjoy the season without feeling caught in the middle.</p>
<h2>Read your parenting plan early</h2><p>Many disputes start because someone assumed the schedule rather than checking it. Review holiday provisions weeks in advance, confirm exchange times in writing, and clarify any ambiguity calmly before plans are made.</p>
<h2>Put the child's experience first</h2><p>Children remember how the holidays felt more than exactly where they spent each day. Avoid scheduling that turns the season into a series of rushed handoffs, and resist the urge to compete with gifts.</p>
<h2>Communicate like colleagues</h2><p>Keep messages brief, factual, and polite. Co-parenting apps can help by keeping a clear record and reducing the temptation to relitigate old arguments.</p>
<h2>Know when to get help</h2><p>If the other parent repeatedly ignores the schedule, or if there are safety concerns, document what happens and talk to an attorney about enforcement or modification options.</p>
<blockquote>The best holiday gift for a child of separated parents is permission to enjoy both homes.</blockquote>'''),
]
